Article 14 An open-ended labor contract refers to a labor contract for which the employing unit and the worker have agreed on no definite termination time.
The employing unit and the worker may conclude an open-ended labor contract through consultation. In any of the following circumstances, if the worker proposes or agrees to renew or conclude a labor contract, an open-ended labor contract shall be concluded, unless the worker proposes the conclusion of a fixed-term labor contract:
(1) The worker has worked continuously for the employing unit for ten years;
(2) When an employing unit first implements a labor contract system or when a state-owned enterprise re-concludes labor contracts as a result of restructuring, the worker has worked continuously for the employing unit for ten years and is less than ten years away from the statutory retirement age;
(3) The worker has concluded fixed-term labor contracts consecutively twice, and is not under the circumstances as specified in Article 39 or Paragraph 1 or 2 of Article 40 of this Law, and the labor contract is to be renewed.
If an employing unit does not conclude a written labor contract with a worker within one year from the date the employment commences, it shall be deemed that the employing unit and the worker have concluded an open-ended labor contract.

Article 17 A labor contract shall contain the following clauses:
(1) The name, domicile and legal representative or principal person in charge of the employing unit;
(2) The name, address and number of the resident identity card or other valid identity document of the worker;
(3) The term of the labor contract;
(4) The job content and work location;
(5) Working hours, rest and vacation;
(6) Labor remuneration;
(7) Social insurance;
(8) Labor protection, working conditions and protection against occupational hazards;
(9) Other matters that laws and regulations require to be included in the labor contract.
In addition to the requisite clauses as specified in the preceding paragraph, the employing unit and the worker may agree on other matters such as probation period, training, confidentiality, supplementary insurance, and welfare benefits.

Article 26 The following labor contracts shall be void or partially void:
(1) Those concluded or modified by means of fraud, coercion or taking advantage of the other party's hardship, causing the other party to act contrary to its true intention;
(2) Those whereby the employing unit exempts itself from legal liability or excludes the rights of the worker;
(3) Those that violate mandatory provisions of laws or administrative regulations.
Any dispute over the voidness or partial voidness of a labor contract shall be determined by a labor dispute arbitration body or a People's Court.

Article 38 A worker may rescind the labor contract if the employing unit falls under any of the following circumstances:
(1) Failing to provide labor protection or working conditions as agreed in the labor contract;
(2) Failing to pay labor remuneration in full and on time;
(3) Failing to pay social insurance premiums for the worker according to law;
(4) The rules and regulations of the employing unit violate laws or regulations and harm the rights and interests of the worker;
(5) The labor contract becomes void due to the circumstances specified in Paragraph 1 of Article 26 of this Law;
(6) Other circumstances under which a worker may rescind the labor contract as provided by laws or administrative regulations.
If the employing unit compels the worker to work by means of violence, threats or illegal restriction of personal freedom, or if the employing unit, by orders in violation of rules or orders to risk operations, endangers the personal safety of the worker, the worker may immediately rescind the labor contract without giving prior notice to the employing unit.

Article 39 The employing unit may rescind the labor contract if the worker falls under any of the following circumstances:
(1) Being proved not to meet the recruitment conditions during the probation period;
(2) Seriously violating the rules and regulations of the employing unit;
(3) Committing serious dereliction of duty, engaging in malpractice for personal gain, thereby causing substantial damage to the employing unit;
(4) The worker simultaneously establishes labor relations with another employing unit, which seriously affects the completion of the work tasks of the original employing unit, or the worker refuses to rectify after the employing unit raises the issue;
(5) The labor contract becomes void due to the circumstances specified in Item (1) of Paragraph 1 of Article 26 of this Law;
(6) Being subject to criminal liability according to law.

Article 40 In any of the following circumstances, the employing unit may rescind the labor contract by giving the worker thirty days' prior written notice or by paying the worker an additional month's wages:
(1) The worker suffers from illness or non-work-related injury and, after the prescribed period of medical treatment, is unable to engage in the original job or any other job arranged by the employing unit;
(2) The worker is incompetent in the job and, after receiving training or being transferred to another position, remains incompetent;
(3) The objective conditions based on which the labor contract was concluded undergo a major change, making the labor contract unperformable, and the employing unit and the worker fail to reach an agreement on amending the contents of the labor contract after consultation.

Article 41 In any of the following circumstances, where it is necessary to reduce the workforce by twenty persons or more, or by less than twenty persons but accounting for more than ten percent of the total number of employees, the employing unit may reduce the workforce after explaining the situation to the trade union or all employees thirty days in advance, soliciting the opinions of the trade union or employees, and reporting the workforce reduction plan to the labor administration department:
(1) Reorganizing in accordance with the Enterprise Bankruptcy Law;
(2) Experiencing serious difficulties in production and operations;
(3) Changing the line of production, undergoing major technological innovation or adjusting the mode of operation, and still needing to reduce the workforce after modifying the labor contracts;
(4) Other major changes in the objective economic circumstances based on which the labor contract was concluded, making the labor contract unperformable.
When reducing the workforce, priority shall be given to retaining the following persons:
(1) Those having concluded fixed-term labor contracts of a relatively long term with the employing unit;
(2) Those having concluded open-ended labor contracts with the employing unit;
(3) Those who are the sole earners in their families with elderly persons or minors to support.
If, within six months after reducing the workforce in accordance with the preceding paragraph, the employing unit recruits personnel again, it shall notify the persons who were laid off and shall, under equal conditions, give priority to hiring the persons who were laid off.

Article 42 The employing unit shall not rescind the labor contract in accordance with Articles 40 or 41 of this Law if the worker falls under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The worker is engaged in work involving exposure to occupational hazards and has not undergone a pre-departure occupational health check, or the worker is a suspected occupational disease patient and is during diagnosis or medical observation;
(2) The worker has contracted an occupational disease or sustained a work-related injury and has been confirmed as having lost or partially lost the capacity to work;
(3) The worker suffers from illness or non-work-related injury and is within the prescribed period of medical treatment;
(4) The worker is a female employee during pregnancy, childbirth or nursing;
(5) The worker has worked continuously for the employing unit for not less than fifteen years and is less than five years away from the statutory retirement age;
(6) Other circumstances provided by laws or administrative regulations.

Article 44 A labor contract shall terminate in any of the following circumstances:
(1) The term of the labor contract expires;
(2) The worker begins to receive basic old-age insurance benefits according to law;
(3) The worker dies, or is declared dead or missing by a People's Court;
(4) The employing unit is declared bankrupt according to law;
(5) The employing unit's business license is revoked, or the employing unit is ordered to close down, is dissolved, or decides to dissolve early;
(6) Other circumstances provided by laws or administrative regulations.

Article 46 The employing unit shall pay economic compensation to the worker in any of the following circumstances:
(1) The worker rescinds the labor contract in accordance with Article 38 of this Law;
(2) The employing unit proposes to rescind the labor contract in accordance with Article 36 of this Law and rescinds it through consultation with the worker;
(3) The employing unit rescinds the labor contract in accordance with Article 40 of this Law;
(4) The employing unit rescinds the labor contract in accordance with Paragraph 1 of Article 41 of this Law;
(5) The fixed-term labor contract is terminated in accordance with Item (1) of Article 44 of this Law, except where the employing unit maintains or improves the conditions of the labor contract for renewal and the worker does not agree to renew;
(6) The labor contract is terminated in accordance with Item (4) or (5) of Article 44 of this Law;
(7) Other circumstances provided by laws or administrative regulations.

Article 74 The labor administrative departments of local people's governments at or above the county level shall, according to law, conduct supervision and inspection of the following circumstances regarding the implementation of the labor contract system:
(1) the formulation by the employer of internal rules and regulations directly involving the immediate interests of workers and the implementation thereof;
(2) the conclusion and rescission of labor contracts by the employer and workers;
(3) the compliance by labor dispatch units and employing entities with relevant provisions on labor dispatch;
(4) the compliance by the employer with state provisions on working hours, rest, and leave of workers;
(5) the payment by the employer of labor remuneration as agreed in the labor contract and the implementation of the minimum wage standard;
(6) the participation by the employer in various social insurances and the payment of social insurance premiums; and
(7) other matters of labor supervision stipulated by laws and regulations.

Article 85 Where an employer is under any of the following circumstances, the labor administrative department shall order the employer to pay labor remuneration, overtime pay, or economic compensation within a time limit; if the labor remuneration is lower than the local minimum wage standard, the employer shall pay the difference; if payment is not made within the time limit, the labor administrative department shall order the employer to pay additional compensation to the worker at a rate of not less than 50% but not more than 100% of the amount payable:
(1) failing to pay labor remuneration to workers in full and on time as stipulated in the labor contract or as provided by the state;
(2) paying wages to workers below the local minimum wage standard;
(3) arranging overtime work without paying overtime pay; or
(4) rescinding or terminating a labor contract without paying economic compensation to the worker in accordance with the provisions of this Law.

Article 88 Where an employer is under any of the following circumstances, an administrative penalty shall be imposed according to law; if a crime is constituted, criminal liability shall be investigated according to law; if damage is caused to workers, the employer shall bear compensation liability:
(1) forcing workers to work by means of violence, threat, or illegal restriction of personal freedom;
(2) directing or forcing workers to operate in violation of regulations or to take risks, thereby endangering the personal safety of workers;
(3) insulting, physically punishing, beating, unlawfully searching, or detaining workers; or
(4) causing serious damage to the physical and mental health of workers due to poor working conditions or serious environmental pollution.
